1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:06,000
한글 자막 — Junwon Lee (romaanj.github.io/talks)
원본: Dwarkesh Patel 채널 (YouTube) · 기계번역 보조, 오류 제보 환영

2
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:50,787
오늘은 안드레이 카파시와
이야기를 나눠보겠습니다.
Today I'm speaking with Andrej Karpathy.

3
00:00:50,787 --> 00:00:53,386
안드레이, 왜 이걸
'에이전트의 해'가 아니라
Andrej, why do you say that this will be the decade of agents and not the year of agents?

4
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:55,778
'에이전트의 10년'이라고
하시는 건가요?

5
00:00:55,778 --> 00:00:58,308
먼저, 이 자리에 초대해
주셔서 감사합니다.
First of all, thank you for having me here.

6
00:00:58,308 --> 00:00:59,598
이렇게 함께하게 되어 기쁘네요.
I'm excited to be here.

7
00:00:59,598 --> 00:01:02,638
방금 말씀하신 '에이전트의 10년'이라는
The quote you've just mentioned, "It's the decade of agents," is actually a reaction to a pre-existing quote.

8
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:06,230
표현은 사실 기존에 있던
말에 대한 반응이에요.

9
00:01:06,230 --> 00:01:11,473
누가 한 말인지는 정확히
모르겠지만, LLM이 앞으로 어떻게
I'm not actually sure who said this but they were alluding to this being the year of agents with respect to LLMs and how they were going to evolve.

10
00:01:11,473 --> 00:01:16,240
진화할지를 두고 '에이전트의
해'라고 암시한 거였어요.

11
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:18,928
그 말에 좀 자극을
받았는데, 업계에 과도한
I was triggered by that because there's some over-prediction going on in the industry.

12
00:01:18,928 --> 00:01:21,280
예측이 퍼져 있다고 느꼈기 때문이에요.

13
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,496
제 생각엔 이건
'에이전트의 10년'이라고
In my mind, this is more accurately described as the decade of agents.

14
00:01:23,496 --> 00:01:25,520
하는 게 더 정확한 표현인 것 같아요.

15
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:29,117
지금도 아주 초기 단계의
에이전트들, 그러니까 Claude나
We have some very early agents that are extremely impressive and that I use daily—Claude and Codex and so on—but I still feel there's so much work to be done.

16
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:32,388
Codex 같은 것들이 정말
인상적이고 저도 매일 쓰고

17
00:01:32,388 --> 00:01:35,440
있지만, 아직 해야 할 일이
정말 많다고 느껴져요.

18
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:36,929
그래서 제 반응은,
우리가 이 녀석들과 앞으로
My reaction is we'll be working with these things for a decade.

19
00:01:36,929 --> 00:01:38,240
10년은 함께 일하게 될 거라는 거예요.

20
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:42,000
계속 더 좋아질 거고, 정말
멋진 일이 될 거예요.
They're going to get better, and it's going to be wonderful.

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,880
저는 그냥 그 말이 함축하는
시간축에 반응한 거였어요.
I was just reacting to the timelines of the implication.

22
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:49,473
그럼 10년이 걸릴 거라고
보시는 건 구체적으로 뭔가요?
What do you think will take a decade to accomplish?

23
00:01:49,473 --> 00:01:51,840
병목은 뭔가요?
What are the bottlenecks?

24
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:53,062
실제로 작동하게 만드는 것, 그 자체요.
Actually making it work.

25
00:01:53,062 --> 00:01:56,119
에이전트라고 할 때, 그러니까
랩들이 염두에 둔 것,
When you're talking about an agent, or what the labs have in mind and maybe what I have in mind as well, you should think of it almost like an employee or an intern that you would hire to work with you.

26
00:01:56,119 --> 00:01:59,176
그리고 아마 저도 마찬가지인데,
이건 거의 같이 일할

27
00:01:59,176 --> 00:02:02,022
직원이나 인턴을 채용하는
것처럼 생각하시면 돼요.

28
00:02:02,022 --> 00:02:04,720
예를 들어 여기서도 몇몇
직원분들과 함께 일하시잖아요.
For example, you work with some employees here.

29
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:06,623
그 일을 Claude나
Codex 같은 에이전트가
When would you prefer to have an agent like Claude or Codex do that work?

30
00:02:06,623 --> 00:02:08,244
대신 하게 하고 싶어지는
건 언제쯤일까요?

31
00:02:08,244 --> 00:02:09,649
지금은 당연히 못 하죠.
Currently, of course they can't.

32
00:02:09,649 --> 00:02:11,760
그게 가능해지려면 뭐가 필요할까요?
What would it take for them to be able to do that?

33
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:12,820
지금은 왜 그렇게 안 하시는 거죠?
Why don't you do it today?

34
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:15,670
지금 안 하시는 이유는, 그냥 아직
제대로 작동을 안 하기 때문이에요.
The reason you don't do it today is because they just don't work.

35
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:18,434
지능이 충분치 않고,
멀티모달 능력도 부족하고,
They don't have enough intelligence, they're not multimodal enough, they can't do computer use and all this stuff.

36
00:02:18,434 --> 00:02:20,880
컴퓨터 사용 같은 것도
제대로 못 하고요.

37
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:23,513
아까 말씀하신 것들 중에도
못 하는 게 많고요.
They don't do a lot of the things you've alluded to earlier.

38
00:02:23,513 --> 00:02:24,909
연속 학습 능력도 없어요.
They don't have continual learning.

39
00:02:24,909 --> 00:02:27,120
그냥 뭔가 말해준다고 그걸
기억하는 게 아니에요.
You can't just tell them something and they'll remember it.

40
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,560
인지적으로 결핍돼 있어서,
그냥 잘 안 되는 거예요.
They're cognitively lacking and it's just not working.

41
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,800
이 모든 문제를 다 해결하는 데
대략 10년 정도 걸릴 거예요.
It will take about a decade to work through all of those issues.

42
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,165
흥미롭네요.
Interesting.

43
00:02:34,165 --> 00:02:38,762
전문 팟캐스터로서, 그리고
AI를 멀리서 지켜보는 사람으로서
As a professional podcaster and a viewer of AI from afar, it's easy for me to identify what's lacking: continual learning is lacking, or multimodality is lacking.

44
00:02:38,762 --> 00:02:42,662
저도 뭐가 부족한지는 쉽게
짚을 수 있어요 — 연속

45
00:02:42,662 --> 00:02:47,120
학습이 부족하다, 멀티모달리티가
부족하다, 이런 식으로요.

46
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:52,720
하지만 거기에 정확히 언제까지라고
못 박을 좋은 방법은 딱히 없어요.
But I don't really have a good way of trying to put a timeline on it.

47
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:57,200
누가 연속 학습이 얼마나 걸릴지
물으면, 이게 5년짜리 프로젝트인지
If somebody asks how long continual learning will take, I have no prior about whether this is a project that should take 5 years, 10 years, or 50 years.

48
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,196
10년짜리인지 50년짜리인지에
대한 사전 감이 전혀 없어요.

49
00:03:01,196 --> 00:03:02,020
왜 10년인가요?
Why a decade?

50
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:03,327
왜 1년은 아닌가요?
Why not one year?

51
00:03:03,327 --> 00:03:04,633
왜 50년은 아닌가요?
Why not 50 years?

52
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:10,034
여기서부터는 제 나름의 직관이
좀 들어가는데요, 이 분야에서의
This is where you get into a bit of my own intuition, and doing a bit of an extrapolation with respect to my own experience in the field.

53
00:03:10,034 --> 00:03:14,640
제 경험을 바탕으로 어느
정도 외삽을 해보는 거예요.

54
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:17,068
저는 거의 20년
가까이 AI를 해왔어요.
I've been in AI for almost two decades.

55
00:03:17,068 --> 00:03:19,920
15년 정도예요, 그렇게 길지는 않고요.
It's going to be 15 years or so, not that long.

56
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,040
여기 리처드 서튼도 나오셨었는데,
그분은 훨씬 더 오래 계셨죠.
You had Richard Sutton here, who was around for much longer.

57
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:25,571
그래도 저는 사람들이
예측을 하고 그게 어떻게
I do have about 15 years of experience of people making predictions, of seeing how they turned out.

58
00:03:25,571 --> 00:03:28,000
됐는지 지켜본 경험이
15년 정도는 있어요.

59
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,247
또 저는 한동안 업계에
있었고, 연구 쪽에도
Also I was in the industry for a while, I was in research, and I've worked in the industry for a while.

60
00:03:30,247 --> 00:03:32,400
있었고, 업계에서
한동안 일하기도 했어요.

61
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:37,760
거기서 남은 전반적인 직관이 있어요.
I have a general intuition that I have left from that.

62
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:40,735
이 문제들이 다룰 수
있고 극복 가능한 것
I feel like the problems are tractable, they're surmountable, but they're still difficult.

63
00:03:40,735 --> 00:03:43,840
같지만, 그래도 여전히
어렵다는 느낌이에요.

64
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:47,040
그냥 평균을 내보면, 제겐
대략 10년 정도로 느껴져요.
If I just average it out, it just feels like a decade to me.

65
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:48,686
이거 꽤 흥미롭네요.
This is quite interesting.

66
00:03:48,686 --> 00:03:53,179
역사 자체뿐 아니라, 여러
돌파구의 순간마다 그 자리에 있던
I want to hear not only the history, but what people in the room felt was about to happen at various different breakthrough moments.

67
00:03:53,179 --> 00:03:58,080
사람들이 앞으로 무슨 일이 일어날
거라고 느꼈는지도 듣고 싶어요.

68
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:00,564
그 느낌들이 어떤 식으로 지나치게
What were the ways in which their feelings were either overly pessimistic or overly optimistic?

69
00:04:00,564 --> 00:04:03,600
비관적이었거나, 지나치게 낙관적이었나요?

70
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,451
하나씩 차례로 짚어볼까요?
Should we just go through each of them one by one?

71
00:04:05,451 --> 00:04:07,312
그건 엄청나게 큰
질문이에요, 15년 동안
That's a giant question because you're talking about 15 years of stuff that happened.

72
00:04:07,312 --> 00:04:08,930
일어난 일들을 다 얘기하는 거니까요.

73
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:13,098
AI가 정말 멋진 이유는, 분야
전체가 갑자기 완전히 다른 모습으로
AI is so wonderful because there have been a number of seismic shifts where the entire field has suddenly looked a different way.

74
00:04:13,098 --> 00:04:17,040
보이게 되는 지각변동 같은 순간들이
여러 번 있었기 때문이에요.

75
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:19,247
저도 아마 그런 걸 두세
번은 겪은 것 같아요.
I've maybe lived through two or three of those.

76
00:04:19,247 --> 00:04:25,200
앞으로도 계속 있을 거라고 봐요,
놀라울 정도로 규칙적으로 찾아오거든요.
I still think there will continue to be some because they come with almost surprising regularity.

77
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,381
제 경력이 시작될 때, 딥러닝을
시작했을 때, 딥러닝에
When my career began, when I started to work on deep learning, when I became interested in deep learning, this was by chance of being right next to Geoff Hinton at the University of Toronto.

78
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:31,455
관심을 갖게 됐을 때, 그건
우연히 토론토 대학교에서

79
00:04:31,455 --> 00:04:34,000
제프리 힌튼 바로 옆에
있었기 때문이었어요.

80
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,040
제프리 힌튼은 물론 AI의
대부 같은 인물이죠.
Geoff Hinton, of course, is the godfather figure of AI.

81
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,582
그는 온갖 신경망들을
훈련시키고 있었어요.
He was training all these neural networks.

82
00:04:38,582 --> 00:04:40,160
저는 그게 정말 놀랍고
흥미롭다고 생각했어요.
I thought it was incredible and interesting.

83
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:43,680
당시 AI 업계 사람들 대부분이
하던 주된 일과는 거리가 멀었죠.
This was not the main thing that everyone in AI was doing by far.

84
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:46,560
그냥 곁가지의 작은 틈새 주제였어요.
This was a niche little subject on the side.

85
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:49,096
그게 아마 AlexNet과 함께 찾아온
That's maybe the first dramatic seismic shift that came with the AlexNet and so on.

86
00:04:49,096 --> 00:04:51,633
첫 번째 극적인 지각변동이었을 거예요.

87
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:55,412
AlexNet이 다들 방향을 바꾸게
했고, 다들 신경망을 훈련시키기
AlexNet reoriented everyone, and everyone started to train neural networks, but it was still very per-task, per specific task.

88
00:04:55,412 --> 00:04:59,600
시작했지만, 그래도 여전히 과제별로,
특정 과제 하나하나에 맞춘 거였어요.

89
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:04,400
예를 들면 이미지 분류기 하나, 신경망
기계번역기 하나, 그런 식이었죠.
Maybe I have an image classifier or I have a neural machine translator or something like that.

90
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,715
사람들은 아주 서서히 에이전트에
관심을 갖기 시작했어요.
People became very slowly interested in agents.

91
00:05:07,715 --> 00:05:11,761
"좋아, 시각피질 부분은 이제
체크했다 치고, 뇌의 나머지
People started to think, "Okay, maybe we have a check mark next to the visual cortex or something like that, but what about the other parts of the brain, and how can we get a full agent or a full entity that can interact in the world?"

92
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:15,554
부분들은? 세상과 상호작용할
수 있는 온전한 에이전트,

93
00:05:15,554 --> 00:05:19,600
온전한 개체는 어떻게 얻지?"
라고 생각하기 시작한 거죠.

94
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:24,554
제 생각엔 2013년쯤 있었던 아타리
심층강화학습으로의 전환이 그 초기
The Atari deep reinforcement learning shift in 2013 or so was part of that early effort of agents, in my mind, because it was an attempt to try to get agents that not just perceive the world, but also take actions and interact and get rewards from environments.

95
00:05:24,554 --> 00:05:29,253
에이전트 노력의 일부였어요, 세상을
지각할 뿐 아니라 행동을 취하고

96
00:05:29,253 --> 00:05:34,080
상호작용하고 환경에서 보상을 받는
에이전트를 만들려는 시도였으니까요.

97
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:36,605
당시엔 그게 아타리 게임이었죠.
At the time, this was Atari games.

98
00:05:36,605 --> 00:05:39,680
저는 그게 헛발질이었다고 생각해요.
I feel that was a misstep.

99
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:44,439
제가 몸담았던 초기 OpenAI조차
받아들였던 헛발질이었어요, 그 시절
It was a misstep that even the early OpenAI that I was a part of adopted because at that time, the zeitgeist was reinforcement learning environments, games, game playing, beat games, get lots of different types of games, and OpenAI was doing a lot of that.

100
00:05:44,439 --> 00:05:49,321
시대정신이 강화학습 환경, 게임, 게임
플레이, 게임 깨기, 온갖 종류의

101
00:05:49,321 --> 00:05:54,080
게임을 확보하는 거였고, OpenAI도
그런 걸 많이 하고 있었거든요.

102
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:58,952
그게 AI의 또 다른 두드러진
부분이었는데, 한 2,
That was another prominent part of AI where maybe for two or three or four years, everyone was doing reinforcement learning on games.

103
00:05:58,952 --> 00:06:04,161
3, 4년 동안은 다들 게임에
강화학습을 하고 있었어요.

104
00:06:04,161 --> 00:06:06,800
그것도 다 조금은 헛발질이었죠.
That was all a bit of a misstep.

105
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:09,200
제가 OpenAI에서
하려던 건... 저는 늘
What I was trying to do at OpenAI is I was always a bit suspicious of games as being this thing that would lead to AGI.

106
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:11,983
게임이 AGI로 이어질
거라는 데 좀 회의적이었어요.

107
00:06:11,983 --> 00:06:14,679
왜냐하면 제 생각엔,
회계사 같은 것, 실제
Because in my mind, you want something like an accountant or something that's interacting with the real world.

108
00:06:14,679 --> 00:06:17,600
세상과 상호작용하는 뭔가가
필요하다고 봤거든요.

109
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:20,480
게임이 어떻게 그런 걸로 이어지는지
도무지 알 수가 없었어요.
I just didn't see how games add up to it.

110
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:23,316
예를 들어 제가 OpenAI에서
했던 프로젝트는
My project at OpenAI, for example, was within the scope of the Universe project, on an agent that was using keyboard and mouse to operate web pages.

111
00:06:23,316 --> 00:06:26,807
유니버스(Universe)
프로젝트 범위 안에서, 키보드와

