왜냐하면 그냥 이리저리 헤매면서
키보드를 마구 두드리고
Because if you're just stumbling your way around and keyboard mashing and mouse clicking and trying to get rewards in these environments, your reward is too sparse and you just won't learn.

118
00:06:45,694 --> 00:06:48,827
마우스를 클릭하면서 이런
환경에서 보상을 얻으려

119
00:06:48,827 --> 00:06:52,080
하면, 보상이 너무 희박해서
학습이 안 되거든요.

120
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:54,626
숲 하나를 통째로 태울
만큼 컴퓨팅을 쏟아붓고도,
You're going to burn a forest computing, and you're never going to get something off the ground.

121
00:06:54,626 --> 00:06:56,794
결국 아무것도 제대로
띄우지 못할 거예요.

122
00:06:56,794 --> 00:07:00,440
그때 빠져 있던 건 신경망이
가진 표현력이라는 힘이었어요.
What you're missing is this power of representation in the neural network.

123
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:02,928
예를 들어 요즘 사람들이
컴퓨터를 사용하는 에이전트를
For example, today people are training those computer-using agents, but they're doing it on top of a large language model.

124
00:07:02,928 --> 00:07:05,760
학습시키는데, 그것도 거대 언어
모델 위에서 하고 있잖아요.

125
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:08,715
언어 모델을 먼저 얻어야 하고,
표현을 먼저 얻어야 하는데,
You have to get the language model first, you have to get the representations first, and you have to do that by all the pre-training and all the LLM stuff.

126
00:07:08,715 --> 00:07:11,760
그건 사전학습과 LLM 관련
작업들을 통해서만 가능한 거예요.

127
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:17,280
대략 말하면, 사람들이 몇 번이고
너무 일찍 완성형을 만들려고
I feel maybe loosely speaking, people kept trying to get the full thing too early a few times, where people really try to go after agents too early, I would say.

128
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:22,800
했던 것 같아요. 에이전트를 정말
너무 일찍부터 좇았던 거죠.

129
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:24,592
아타리도 그랬고,
유니버스(Universe)도
That was Atari and Universe and even my own experience.

130
00:07:24,592 --> 00:07:26,240
그랬고, 심지어 제
경험도 마찬가지였어요.

131
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:30,560
그런 에이전트에 도달하려면 실제로
먼저 해둬야 할 것들이 있는 거예요.
You actually have to do some things first before you get to those agents.

132
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:33,268
지금은 에이전트가 훨씬
더 유능해졌지만, 그래도
Now the agents are a lot more competent, but maybe we're still missing some parts of that stack.

133
00:07:33,268 --> 00:07:36,080
그 스택의 일부는 여전히
빠져 있을 수도 있어요.

134
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:40,899
사람들이 하던 일을 크게 세
갈래로 나눌 수 있을 것 같아요.
I would say those are the three major buckets of what people were doing: training neural nets per-tasks, trying the first round of agents, and then maybe the LLMs and seeking the representation power of the neural networks before you tack on everything else on top.

135
00:07:40,899 --> 00:07:46,143
과제별로 신경망을 학습시키던 것,
첫 번째 에이전트 시도들, 그리고

136
00:07:46,143 --> 00:07:51,103
다른 걸 얹기 전에 신경망의
표현력부터 추구했던 LLM 단계요.

137
00:07:51,103 --> 00:07:51,847
흥미롭네요.
Interesting.

138
00:07:51,847 --> 00:07:54,498
서튼의 관점을 최대한
옹호해보자면, 인간은 그냥
If I were to steelman the Sutton perspective, it would be that humans can just take on everything at once, or even animals can take on everything at once.

139
00:07:54,498 --> 00:07:57,148
모든 걸 한 번에 해낼
수 있다는 거고, 심지어

140
00:07:57,148 --> 00:07:59,901
동물도 모든 걸 한 번에
해낼 수 있다는 거예요.

141
00:07:59,901 --> 00:08:03,682
동물이 오히려 더 나은 예일 수도
있는데, 언어라는 발판조차 없으니까요.
Animals are maybe a better example because they don't even have the scaffold of language.

142
00:08:03,682 --> 00:08:06,914
그냥 세상에 던져져서, 아무 라벨도 없이
They just get thrown out into the world, and they just have to make sense of everything without any labels.

143
00:08:06,914 --> 00:08:10,000
모든 걸 스스로 파악해내야 하는 거죠.

144
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,274
그럼 AGI의 비전은 그냥 감각
데이터를 보고, 컴퓨터 화면을
The vision for AGI then should just be something which looks at sensory data, looks at the computer screen, and it just figures out what's going on from scratch.

145
00:08:14,274 --> 00:08:18,800
보고, 처음부터 스스로 상황을
파악해내는 존재여야 한다는 거고요.

146
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:21,722
인간을 비슷한 상황에 놓고
처음부터 학습시켜야 한다면…
If a human were put in a similar situation and had to be trained from scratch…

147
00:08:21,722 --> 00:08:24,166
이건 인간이 자라나는 것, 혹은
동물이 자라나는 것과 같은 거예요.
This is like a human growing up or an animal growing up.

148
00:08:24,166 --> 00:08:27,097
수백만 년치 학습을 시키는
지금 방식 대신, 그게
Why shouldn't that be the vision for AI, rather than this thing where we're doing millions of years of training?

149
00:08:27,097 --> 00:08:29,920
AI의 비전이 되지 말아야
할 이유가 있을까요?

150
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:31,992
정말 좋은 질문이네요.
That's a really good question.

151
00:08:31,992 --> 00:08:36,946
서튼이 이 팟캐스트에 나오셨었죠,
그 방송을 봤고 그에 대한
Sutton was on your podcast and I saw the podcast and I had a write-up about that podcast that gets into a bit of how I see things.

152
00:08:36,946 --> 00:08:41,600
글도 썼는데, 거기서 제가
어떻게 보는지 조금 다뤘어요.

153
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:44,753
저는 동물에 빗대는 데 아주
조심스러운 편인데, 동물은
I'm very careful to make analogies to animals because they came about by a very different optimization process.

154
00:08:44,753 --> 00:08:48,116
완전히 다른 최적화 과정을 거쳐
나온 존재이기 때문이에요.

155
00:08:48,116 --> 00:08:52,779
동물은 진화한 존재라서, 엄청난 양의
하드웨어가 이미 내장돼 있어요.
Animals are evolved, and they come with a huge amount of hardware that's built in.

156
